\begin_layout Section
Guiding-center Motion in Axisymmetric Dipole geometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin with the expression for a pure-dipole magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{B}=\frac{\mathcal{M}}{r^{3}}\left(2\cos\theta\wh r+\sin\theta\wh{\theta}\right),\label{eq:Bdip_def}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where spherical coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r,\theta,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 are used and the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}\equiv B_{\mathrm{e}}r_{\mathrm{e}}^{3}$
\end_inset

 combines the strength of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 at the equatorial radial distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 (at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\pi/2$
\end_inset

).
 We can also write the magnetic field 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Bdip_def"

\end_inset

 in 2-covariant flux-coordinate representation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}=\nabla\psi\btimes\nabla\phi$
\end_inset

, where the poloidal flux 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\psi\left(r,\theta\right)=\frac{\mathcal{M}}{r}\sin^{2}\theta.\label{eq:psi_def}\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since a magnetic field line lies on a constant-
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 surface (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}\bdot\nabla\psi=0$
\end_inset

), we find that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
r\left(\theta\right)=r_{\mathrm{e}}\sin^{2}\theta\label{eq:r_theta}\end{equation}

\end_inset

on a single magnetic field line (with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}\equiv\psi_{\mathrm{e}}r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

), where the equatorial radial distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 (at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\pi/2$
\end_inset

) can be used as a field label.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:r_theta"

\end_inset

 allows us to introduce the distance along a magnetic field line (labeled
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
s\left(r_{\mathrm{e}},\theta\right)\equiv r_{\mathrm{e}}\int_{\frac{\pi}{2}}^{\theta}\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\theta^{\prime}}\sin\theta^{\prime}d\theta^{\prime},\label{eq:s_def}\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is defined to be zero positive for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta>\pi/2$
\end_inset

 (below the equator), negative for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\pi/2$
\end_inset

 (above the equator), and zero at the equator 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\theta=\pi/2\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The integral in Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:s_def"

\end_inset

 can be evaluated explicitly, and we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
s\left(r_{\mathrm{e}},\theta\right)=\frac{r_{\mathrm{e}}}{2\sqrt{3}}\left[\ln\left(\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\theta}-\sqrt{3}\cos\theta\right)-\sqrt{3}\cos\theta\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\theta}\right].\label{eq:s_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

The magnetic-field strength on a single line, on the other hand, is given
 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
B\left(\theta\right)\equiv B_{\mathrm{e}}\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\theta}/\sin^{6}\theta,\label{eq:B_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

which clearly shows that, in this pure-dipole model, all particles are trapped
 (since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 becomes infinite as 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

).
 We note that, by combining Eqs.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:s_dipole"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:B_dipole"

\end_inset

, the magnitude of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(s\right)$
\end_inset

 can be represented parametrically as a function of the parallel spatial
 coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 The parallel unit vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat$
\end_inset

 is expressed in terms of the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\psi,s,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bhat=\nabla s+a\left(\psi,s\right)\nabla\psi,\label{eq:bhat_psis}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a\left(\psi,s\right)\equiv\bhat\bdot\partial\mathbf{X}/\partial\psi$
\end_inset

.
 The curl of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat$
\end_inset

 is, therefore, expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla\btimes\bhat=\pd as\nabla s\btimes\nabla\psi\equiv\pd as\bhat\btimes\nabla\psi,\label{eq:curl_bhat_psis}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the magnetic curvature of a dipole field is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bhat\bdot\nabla\bhat=\pd as\nabla\psi.\label{eq:curv_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

The magnetic-field gradient can be expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla B\left(\psi,s\right)=\pd B{\psi}\nabla\psi+\pd Bs\nabla s\equiv\left(\pd B{\psi}-a\pd Bs\right)\nabla\psi+\pd Bs\bhat,\label{eq:gradB_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

in terms of its perpendicular components 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\partial B/\partial\psi-a\partial B/\partial s\right)\nabla\psi$
\end_inset

 and its parallel component 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\partial B/\partial s\right)\bhat$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat\btimes\nabla\psi$
\end_inset

 is directed in the toroidal direction only.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, the modified magnetic field 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Bstar"

\end_inset

 is expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{B}^{*}=\left(\nabla\phi+\frac{p_{\|}c}{qB}\pd as\mathbf{B}\right)\btimes\nabla\psi,\label{eq:Bstar_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\|}^{*}\equiv B$
\end_inset

, and the guiding-center velocity 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:xdot_gc"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{\mathbf{X}}=\frac{p_{\|}}{mB}\left(\nabla\phi+\frac{p_{\|}c}{qB}\pd as\mathbf{B}\right)\btimes\nabla\psi-\frac{c\mu}{qB}\left(\nabla\psi\bdot\nabla\ln B\right)\nabla\phi,\label{eq:xdotgc_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\nabla\psi\bdot\nabla\ln B=|\nabla\psi|^{2}\left(\pd{\ln B}{\psi}-a\pd{\ln B}s\right).\]

\end_inset

The components of the guiding-center velocity 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:xdotgc_dip"

\end_inset

 are therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\dot{\psi}\\
\dot{s}\\
\dot{\phi}\end{array}\right)=\dot{\mathbf{X}}\bdot\nabla\left(\begin{array}{c}
\psi\\
s\\
\phi\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
p_{\|}/m\\
\omega_{\mathrm{d}}\end{array}\right),\label{eq:psisphi_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the guiding-center toroidal drift frequency is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\omega_{\mathrm{d}}\equiv\frac{c}{q}\left[\mu\left(\pd B{\psi}-a\pd Bs\right)+\frac{p_{\|}^{2}}{m}\pd as\right].\label{eq:omega_phi}\end{equation}

\end_inset

When the energy coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{E}$
\end_inset

 is used instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\|}$
\end_inset

, the drift frequency 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:omega_phi"

\end_inset

 may be rewritten as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{\phi}=\frac{c\mu}{q}\pd B{\psi}+\frac{p_{\|}}{m}\pd{}s\left(a\frac{cp_{\|}}{q}\right).\label{eq:phi_dot}\end{equation}

\end_inset

The guiding-center parallel acceleration, on the other hand, is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{p}_{\|}=-\mu\mathbf{B}^{*}\bdot\nabla\ln B=-\mu\pd Bs.\label{eq:vdot_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

Lastly, the invariance of the magnetic flux allows us to introduce the drift
 action 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{\mathrm{d}}\equiv q\psi/c$
\end_inset

, which is canonically conjugate to the toroidal angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 in the guiding-center phase-space Lagrangian 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\frac{q}{c}\mathbf{A}+p_{\|}\bhat\right)\bdot\exd\mathbf{X}=\frac{q}{c}\psi\nabla\phi\bdot\exd\mathbf{X}+p_{\|}\left(\nabla s+a\nabla\psi\right)\bdot\exd\mathbf{X}=\frac{q}{c}\psi\exd\phi+p_{\|}\left(\exd s+a\exd\psi\right).\label{eq:drift_action}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Bounce-center motion in axisymmertic dipole geometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, we will derive bounce-angle independant dynamics for particle
 motion through the same Lie transform formalism.
 This transformation makes use of the bounce and drift time scale separation,
 described by the small parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}\equiv\tau_{\mathrm{b}}/\tau_{\mathrm{d}}$
\end_inset

.
 The previous section assumed that nonparallel spatial coordinates were
 frozen ; we will now consider the 4-dimensional phase space 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{\alpha}\equiv\left(\psi,\phi,J,\zeta\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The aim is to derive bounce coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\ov z^{\alpha}\equiv\left(\ov{\psi},\ov{\phi},\ov J,\ov{\zeta}\right)$
\end_inset

 independant of the bounce motion.
 The perturbative analysis will provide corrections to the base coordinates
 at higher order in 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\ov z^{\alpha}=z^{\alpha}+\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}\mathsf{G}_{1}^{\alpha}+\ldots\label{eq:bc_zalpha}\end{equation}

\end_inset

We start from the guiding-center particle Lagrangian, up to an exact derivative,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\Gamma=\left(\frac{q}{c\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}}\mathbf{A}+p_{\|}\bhat\right)\bdot\exd\mathbf{X}-H\exd t\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the particle Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
H=\frac{p_{\|}^{2}}{2m}+\mu B.\label{eq:H}\end{equation}

\end_inset

By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 is independant of the flux coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

, so the Lagrangian can be written using Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:drift_action"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\Gamma=\frac{q}{c\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}}\psi\exd\phi+ap_{\|}\exd\psi+p_{\|}\left(\pd sJ\exd J+\pd s{\zeta}\exd\zeta\right)-H\exd t\equiv\frac{1}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}}\Gamma_{0}+\Gamma_{1}.\end{equation}

\end_inset

The analytical expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $s\left(J,\zeta\right)$
\end_inset

 isn't known in the general case; further approximations are needed in order
 to obtain explicit correction terms fot the bounce-center approximation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first-order generating vector components are calculated by performing
 the time derivative of Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:bc_zalpha"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{\ov z}^{\alpha}=\dot{z}^{\alpha}+\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}\dot{\zeta}\pd{\mathsf{G}_{1}^{\alpha}}{\zeta}+\ldots=\dot{z}^{\alpha}+\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}\omega_{\mathrm{b}}\pd{\mathsf{G}_{1}^{\alpha}}{\zeta}+\ldots,\label{eq:bc_dotzalpha}\end{equation}

\end_inset

which translates to
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathsf{G}_{1}^{\alpha}=\frac{1}{\omega_{\mathrm{b}}}\int\left(\left\langle \dot{z}^{\alpha}\right\rangle _{\mathrm{b}}-\dot{z}^{\alpha}\right)d\zeta,\label{eq:G1alpha}\end{equation}

\end_inset

since the corrections are of higher order in 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{\mathrm{d}}$
\end_inset

.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \dot{z}^{\alpha}\right\rangle _{\mathrm{b}}\equiv\dot{\ov z}^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

is the bounce-angle independant coordinate.
 We can easily deduce 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{G}_{1}^{\psi}=0$
\end_inset

 due to the magnetic flux invariance.
 In order to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{G}_{1}^{\phi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{G}_{1}^{J}$
\end_inset

, we need to have explicit bounce-angle dependant expressions for particle
 motion which are not available in the general case.
 However, we have derived in the next section explicit bounce action-angle
 dependant coordinates for the deeply-trapped approximation.
\end_layout

