\begin_layout Section
Bounce action-angle coordinates for deeply-trapped particles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will now investigate trapped-particle guiding-center motion in the vicinity
 of the equator 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\theta\simeq\pi/2\right)$
\end_inset

.
 For this purpose, we introduce the 
\shape italic
latitude
\shape default
 angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\equiv\theta-\pi/2$
\end_inset

 (with 
\begin_inset Formula $|\sigma|\ll\pi/2$
\end_inset

), so that the distance along a field line 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:s_dipole"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
s= & \frac{r_{\mathrm{e}}}{2\sqrt{3}}\left[\ln\left(\sqrt{1+3\sin^{2}\sigma}+\sqrt{3}\sin\sigma\right)+\sqrt{3}\sin\sigma\sqrt{1+3\sin^{2}\sigma}\right]\nonumber \\
= & r_{\mathrm{e}}\left(\sin\sigma+\frac{1}{2}\sin^{3}\sigma+\cdots\right)=r_{\mathrm{e}}\left(\sigma+\frac{1}{3}\sigma^{3}+\cdots\right),\label{eq:s_sigma}\end{align}

\end_inset

i.e., in the vicinity of the equator, 
\begin_inset Formula $|\sigma|=|s|/r_{\mathrm{e}}\ll1$
\end_inset

 represents a normalized parallel distance.
 In this approximation, the magnetic-field strength 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:B_dipole"

\end_inset

 yields the expression 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{B\left(\theta\right)}{B_{\mathrm{e}}}=\frac{\sqrt{1+3\sin^{2}\sigma}}{\cos^{6}\sigma}=1+\frac{9}{2}\sin^{2}\sigma+\frac{75}{8}\sin^{4}\sigma+\cdots=1+\frac{9}{2}\sigma^{2}+\frac{63}{8}\sigma^{4}+\cdots.\label{eq:B_sigma}\end{equation}

\end_inset

The particle energy therefore reads
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}=\frac{p_{\|}^{2}}{2m}+\mu B_{\mathrm{e}}\left(1+\frac{9}{2}\sigma^{2}+\cdots\right)\label{eq:E_sigma}\end{equation}

\end_inset

Hence, close to the equator, the guiding-center moves in the potential 
\begin_inset Formula $9\mu B_{\mathrm{e}}\sigma^{2}/2$
\end_inset

 of a harmonic oscillator with energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{E}-\mu B_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A deeply-trapped particle orbit is characterized by a pitch-angle coordinate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi\equiv p_{\|}/p$
\end_inset

 whose magnitude is much smaller than unity.
 If we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 as the equatorial pitch-angle coordinate, then conservation of magnetic
 moment yields the relation 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{1-\xi^{2}\left(\theta\right)}{B\left(\theta\right)}=\frac{1-\xi_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}{B_{\mathrm{e}}}.\label{eq:xi_theta}\end{equation}

\end_inset

Hence, for a fixed value 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

, the turning-point angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

 is a solution of the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(\theta_{\mathrm{b}}\right)/B_{\mathrm{e}}\equiv1/\left(1-\xi_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 For deeply-trapped particles with turning points close to the equator (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|\ll1$
\end_inset

), we find 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\mathrm{b}}^{\pm}\equiv\pi/2\pm\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

, and the equatorial pitch-angle coordinate is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|\equiv\frac{3}{\sqrt{2}}\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}\left(1-\frac{11}{8}\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}^{2}+\cdots\right).\label{eq:sigma_b}\end{equation}

\end_inset

Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:eq_pitch"

\end_inset

 shows the equatorial pitch-angle 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

, where we clearly see that, for deeply-trapped particles 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}\ll\pi/2\right)$
\end_inset

, the equatorial pitch-angle 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|$
\end_inset

 depends linearly on 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

